DOCKER_DAYS = {
61:{"title":"Why Containers","phase":"Docker","status":"published","summary":"Containers package an application with its userspace dependencies while isolating processes on a shared host kernel, reducing environment drift without pretending each workload is a virtual machine.","architecture":"application + userspace dependencies → image → container namespaces/cgroups → shared host kernel","how":["An application can fail even when its source code did not change because the runtime environment changed: library versions, system packages, configuration or filesystem assumptions drifted.","A container image packages the userspace filesystem and application dependencies into a repeatable artifact. A running container adds process, network and mount isolation around that image.","The efficiency difference from a virtual machine is fundamental: containers normally share the host kernel instead of booting a guest kernel for every workload. Namespaces isolate views of processes, networking, mounts, hostname and IPC while cgroups can constrain resources."],"commands":"docker version\ndocker run --rm alpine:latest uname -a\ndocker run --rm alpine:latest cat /etc/os-release","verify":"Compare the userspace distribution reported inside the container with the host while observing that the running container uses the host kernel family. Inspect namespace/process isolation with `docker inspect` and host process tools.","gotcha":"A container is not a tiny VM. The shared-kernel model is both a major efficiency advantage and part of the security boundary you must understand.","security":"Use trusted/minimal images, keep the host kernel patched and avoid granting containers unnecessary host namespaces, devices or privileges.","lesson":"Containers solve packaging and isolation problems; they do not erase the host or its kernel.","evidence":"Published journey content and project artifact continuity."},
62:{"title":"Docker Architecture","phase":"Docker","status":"published-artifact","summary":"Docker is a client-server system: the CLI sends API requests, the daemon manages Docker objects and registries distribute images.","architecture":"Docker CLI → Docker API/socket → dockerd → container runtime / images / networks / volumes\n                                ↕\n                              registry","how":["The `docker` command is the client. It parses instructions such as `docker run`, `docker ps` and `docker build` and sends API requests rather than creating containers itself.","`dockerd` is the persistent daemon that manages images, containers, networks and volumes and coordinates lower-level runtime operations.","Registries store and distribute images. When `docker run nginx` needs an image that is not available locally, the daemon can pull it from the configured registry before creating and starting the container."],"commands":"docker version\ndocker info\nsudo systemctl status docker\nls -l /var/run/docker.sock","verify":"Use `docker version` to distinguish client and server information, then stop or inspect the daemon in a safe lab and observe how client-side information can still appear before a daemon connection failure.","gotcha":"Access to `/var/run/docker.sock` is effectively privileged. Members of the `docker` group can control the daemon and commonly gain root-equivalent host capabilities.","security":"Do not expose an unauthenticated Docker API on the network. Protect remote daemon access with SSH or TLS and tightly control socket/group membership.","lesson":"When Docker fails, identify whether the client, daemon, image/registry, runtime or host layer failed instead of blaming one mysterious box called Docker.","evidence":"Published Day 62 infographic artifact recovered from the project archive."},
63:{"title":"Docker Images","phase":"Docker","status":"published","summary":"Docker images are content-addressed, layered filesystem artifacts used as immutable bases for containers; tags are movable labels while digests identify exact image content.","architecture":"Dockerfile / pull → image layers → tag or digest → container writable layer","how":["Image layers allow filesystem content to be reused between images and builds. A later layer does not rewrite an earlier layer; it describes another filesystem change layered on top.","Tags such as `1.0` or `latest` are human-friendly references and can be moved to new image content. A digest identifies a particular manifest/content address and is therefore stronger when reproducibility requires exact identity.","Build cache effectiveness depends on instruction order. Expensive stable dependency steps should generally occur before frequently changing application source when that ordering is correct for the build."],"commands":"docker image ls\ndocker image inspect nginx:alpine\ndocker history nginx:alpine\ndocker pull nginx:alpine","verify":"Inspect the image ID/digest before and after a pull or rebuild and compare `docker history` output. Prove that a tag can refer to different content over time while an immutable digest identifies one version.","gotcha":"`latest` is just a tag. It is not proof that an image is newest, patched or even related to the most recent release.","security":"Prefer trusted registries and pinned digests for sensitive/reproducible deployments, scan images, remove unnecessary packages and avoid baking credentials into layers because deleting them in a later layer does not remove them from earlier image history.","lesson":"Reproducibility depends on image identity, not the comforting English meaning of a tag name.","evidence":"Published topic confirmed by Day 62 teaser and subsequent journey continuity; expanded technical record."},
64:{"title":"Docker Container Lifecycle","phase":"Docker","status":"published-artifact","summary":"A container combines an image with runtime configuration, a primary process and a writable layer whose lifecycle differs from both the image and durable storage.","architecture":"image → create/start container → PID 1 runs → process exits → container stopped → start again OR remove writable layer","how":["A container remains running while its primary process remains alive. When that process exits, the container moves to an exited state even though the image still exists.","`docker stop` requests graceful termination, normally beginning with SIGTERM before a later forced signal if the process does not exit. `docker kill` sends a forceful signal by default and bypasses graceful application shutdown.","Stopping is not removing. Starting the same stopped container preserves its writable layer; removing the container discards that writable layer while leaving the underlying image unchanged."],"commands":"docker run -d --name core-shield-web nginx:alpine\ndocker logs core-shield-web\ndocker exec -it core-shield-web sh\ndocker stop core-shield-web\ndocker start core-shield-web\ndocker rm core-shield-web\ndocker run --rm busybox:stable echo 'temporary task'","verify":"Create a disposable file in a container writable layer, stop/start the same container and confirm it remains. Then remove/recreate the container from the same image and confirm that writable-layer state is gone.","gotcha":"Shell-form ENTRYPOINT or CMD can place the application behind `/bin/sh`, which may not forward termination signals correctly. PID 1 and signal handling matter for clean shutdown.","security":"Do not store secrets or durable application state in the writable layer. Scope `docker exec` access because entering a running container is operationally powerful.","lesson":"Image, container and volume have different lifecycles. Reliability starts when you stop treating them as one thing.","evidence":"Published Day 64 infographic artifacts recovered from the project archive."},
65:{"title":"Docker Volumes","phase":"Docker","status":"published","summary":"Volumes keep important state outside an individual container's writable layer so container replacement does not have to mean data loss.","architecture":"container mount path → named volume or bind mount → host-managed/storage path","how":["A named volume is managed by Docker and attached to containers by name. A bind mount maps a specific host path into the container, which gives the container direct dependence on that host filesystem layout.","The key lifecycle change is that a volume can remain after a container is stopped or removed. A replacement container can mount the same volume and continue using the stored data.","Volumes are especially important for databases and application data. They are not backup systems by themselves; durability against container replacement is different from recovery after deletion, corruption or host loss."],"commands":"docker volume create app-data\ndocker volume ls\ndocker run --rm -v app-data:/data alpine sh -c 'echo hello > /data/example.txt'\ndocker run --rm -v app-data:/data alpine cat /data/example.txt\ndocker volume inspect app-data","verify":"Write data from one disposable container, remove it, mount the same named volume into a new container and prove the data remains.","gotcha":"A named volume surviving container removal does not mean it survives `docker volume rm`, destructive Compose cleanup with volumes, host loss or storage corruption.","security":"Control what paths are mounted and with what access. Bind-mounting sensitive host directories can give a compromised container a path directly into the host filesystem.","lesson":"Separate application state from compute lifecycle, then design backup and recovery as separate concerns.","evidence":"Day 64 published teaser promised Docker Volumes for Day 65 and the completed journey confirms the topic; expanded technical record."},
66:{"title":"Docker Networks","phase":"Docker","status":"published-artifact","summary":"User-defined Docker networks let containers discover one another by names or aliases instead of coupling applications to replaceable container IP addresses.","architecture":"app container → user-defined bridge DNS → db container\nexternal client → published host port → app container","how":["On a single Docker host, a user-defined bridge network provides an isolated L2/L3 network and Docker-managed name resolution for attached containers.","The published lab created `app-net`, started a database named `db` and an application named `app` on that network. The application could reach the database at `db:5432` without knowing the database container's current IP.","Port publishing solves a different problem. `-p 8080:8000` exposes the application's container port through the host; the database does not need a public host port merely because the application needs to reach it internally."],"commands":"docker network ls\ndocker network create app-net\ndocker run -d --name db --network app-net -e POSTGRES_PASSWORD=demo-only postgres:17-alpine\ndocker run -d --name app --network app-net -p 8080:8000 myapp:1.0\ndocker network inspect app-net","verify":"Recreate the database container on the same user-defined network and confirm the application can continue using the `db` name rather than a hard-coded IP. Inspect network membership and DNS behavior.","gotcha":"Automatic container-name DNS is a feature of user-defined networks; do not assume every Docker network mode has identical service-discovery behavior.","security":"Do not publish internal services to the host unless external access is required. Network segmentation reduces accidental reachability but is not an authentication system.","lesson":"Names and aliases are application dependencies; ephemeral container IPs are implementation details.","evidence":"Published Day 66 infographic artifact and post content."},
67:{"title":"Docker Compose","phase":"Docker","status":"published-artifact","summary":"Docker Compose describes a multi-container application, its network, storage, environment and startup relationships in one version-controlled file.","architecture":"compose.yaml → db + app + network + volume + health relationship","how":["The published stack defined PostgreSQL and an application together, attached persistent `db-data`, supplied application connection data and mapped the application to a host port.","A database health check used `pg_isready`, and the application dependency used `condition: service_healthy` rather than assuming process start meant service readiness.","Compose reduces command-memory debt. The point is not merely fewer terminal lines; the system topology becomes inspectable in one declarative artifact."],"commands":"docker compose config\ndocker compose up -d\ndocker compose ps\ndocker compose logs -f\ndocker compose down","verify":"Run `docker compose config` to inspect the resolved configuration, start the stack, confirm database health and make a real application request. Restart or recreate services and verify storage/network behavior remains correct.","gotcha":"`depends_on` startup ordering does not by itself mean the dependency is ready to serve requests. Health-aware conditions are needed when application startup truly depends on readiness.","security":"Keep real credentials outside committed Compose files, limit host port bindings and avoid mounting the Docker socket into services unless the threat model explicitly accepts host-level control.","lesson":"A multi-container application becomes easier to reproduce when topology is code instead of shell history.","evidence":"Published Day 67 infographic artifact."},
68:{"title":"Dockerfiles","phase":"Docker","status":"published-artifact","summary":"A Dockerfile is an ordered build recipe that turns source and dependencies into an image layer by layer.","architecture":"build context + Dockerfile → ordered build instructions/layers → image → container","how":["The published Python example used a slim Python base, set `/app` as the working directory, copied `requirements.txt`, installed dependencies, copied the application source, documented port 8000 and defined the default command.","Instruction order affects cache reuse. Copying dependency metadata before frequently changing source allows the dependency-install layer to remain cached when application files change but requirements do not.","`EXPOSE` documents an expected container port; it does not publish the port to the host. Runtime port publishing remains a separate decision."],"commands":"FROM python:3.12-slim\nWORKDIR /app\nCOPY requirements.txt .\nRUN pip install --no-cache-dir -r requirements.txt\nCOPY . .\nEXPOSE 8000\nCMD [\"python\", \"app.py\"]\n\n# Build / inspect\ndocker build -t myapp:1.0 .\ndocker history myapp:1.0","verify":"Build twice with no source changes and observe cache reuse, then change application source without requirements and confirm dependency installation can remain cached when the Dockerfile is ordered appropriately.","gotcha":"`COPY` reads from the Docker build context, not arbitrary host paths. A file can exist on your computer and still be unavailable to a build whose context does not include it.","security":"Use `.dockerignore`, avoid copying credentials/build secrets into layers, pin dependencies/base images appropriately and run the final application as a non-root user when possible.","lesson":"Dockerfiles are build systems. Layer order, context and defaults affect both reproducibility and attack surface.","evidence":"Published Day 68 infographic artifact."},
69:{"title":"Container Security","phase":"Docker","status":"published","summary":"Container security reduces what a compromised application can do by removing unnecessary identity, filesystem, capability and host privileges.","architecture":"image controls + runtime controls → least-privileged container → limited host impact","how":["The application should run as a non-root identity when it does not require root. A Dockerfile can create an application account and switch to it with `USER`, then runtime identity can be checked with `id`.","A read-only root filesystem prevents writes to most of the container filesystem. If the application needs writable state, provide only the required path with a volume or tmpfs instead of making the entire root filesystem writable.","Linux capabilities should be dropped aggressively and added back only when the workload needs a specific capability. `--privileged` removes too many isolation controls to be a casual compatibility switch."],"commands":"docker run --rm myapp:1.0 id\ndocker run --read-only myapp:1.0\ndocker run --cap-drop=ALL myapp:1.0","verify":"Check the runtime UID/GID, attempt an unnecessary write to the root filesystem, inspect capabilities and run the application's legitimate workload to ensure the reduced privileges still meet requirements.","gotcha":"When a security control breaks a legitimate write, the answer is usually a scoped writable mount or tmpfs, not restoring write access to the entire container filesystem.","security":"Also protect secrets, avoid host namespace/device mounts, keep images patched/minimal and combine container controls with host/kernel/runtime security.","lesson":"A container is isolated, not magically trusted. Design the privilege inherited after compromise.","evidence":"Published Day 69 post preserved in project history."},
70:{"title":"Multi-Container Deployment","phase":"Docker","status":"published-artifact","summary":"The Docker capstone connected image build, private networking, persistent storage, health checks, secrets and runtime hardening into one small application system.","architecture":"client → 127.0.0.1:8080 → app → backend network → PostgreSQL → db-data volume\n                                           ↘ secret file","how":["The published stack built the application locally, ran PostgreSQL from a pinned image, used a named volume for database data and delivered the database password through a Compose secret file instead of a plain environment value.","The application host port was bound to loopback, reducing unintended external exposure, while the database lived only on the backend network.","Database health was part of application startup semantics. Compose then became the capstone artifact connecting concepts from images, containers, networks, volumes, Dockerfiles and container security."],"commands":"docker compose config\ndocker compose up -d --build\ndocker compose ps\ndocker compose logs -f\ndocker compose down","verify":"Make a real request to the application, verify database persistence after service recreation, inspect network membership and confirm the secret value is not committed in the Compose file or printed into logs.","gotcha":"`depends_on` can control startup ordering, but 'started' is not 'ready.' A health condition is required when the application must wait for the database to become usable.","security":"Keep the database unpublished, bind external-facing ports intentionally, use non-root/read-only/capability controls where the workload permits and manage production secrets with a suitable secret system.","lesson":"The phase is complete when storage, communication, startup and privilege are deliberate parts of one system rather than isolated Docker commands.","evidence":"Published Day 70 infographic artifact; Docker phase capstone."}
}
